# A polymorphic function handed to a function nobody annotated either.
#
# `id` is worked out at its first call and comes out polymorphic: its
# body never asks what `x` is. Handing it to `map` is handing over a
# value that is `(Int) -> Int` and `(String) -> String` and every other
# instance at once, so what `map` is worth must not be narrowed to the
# one this call happens to want.
#
# What `map` asks of `f` is that it be applied to an element, once. That
# is `(a) -> b`, whatever was passed -- and it is the same `(a) -> b`
# whether `id` or `show` gets there first, which is the point of the
# three calls below: a definition is worth what its body asks for, not
# what the order its callers are written in makes of it.

def id(x): x

def map(f, lst):
    match lst:
        case [x] + xs: [f(x)] + map(f, xs)
        case []: []

def show(n): 'n=' + string(n)

print(id(4), id('hi'))
print(map(id, [1, 2, 3]))
print(map(show, [4, 5]))
